{"page":850,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.04911,"w":0.25475,"h":0.01851,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"836 Chapter 14: Partial Derivatives"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.09051,"w":0.19641,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"The gradient of ƒ at (-2, 1) is"},{"x":0.48127,"y":0.09321,"w":0.0966,"h":0.07735,"fs":0.01353,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"∇ƒ0 (-2,1) = ax"},{"x":0.57013,"y":0.09815,"w":0.10076,"h":0.06906,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"2 i + 2yjb (-2,1)"},{"x":0.67821,"y":0.12504,"w":0.07692,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"= -i + 2 j."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.16085,"w":0.30139,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The tangent to the ellipse at (-2, 1) is the line"},{"x":0.51391,"y":0.17033,"w":0.29053,"h":0.04612,"fs":0.0243,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"(-1)(x + 2) + (2)(y - 1) = 0 Eq. (6) x - 2y = -4.\b"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.23054,"w":0.59294,"h":0.06031,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"If we know the gradients of two functions ƒ and g, we automatically know the gradients of their sum, difference, constant multiples, product, and quotient. You are asked to establish the following rules in Exercise 40. Notice that these rules have the same form as the cor- responding rules for derivatives of single-variable functions."},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.32102,"w":0.19816,"h":0.01475,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Algebra Rules for Gradients"},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.34345,"w":0.43822,"h":0.07521,"fs":0.02542,"n":8,"k":1,"t":"1. Sum Rule: ∇(ƒ + g) = ∇ƒ + ∇g 2. Difference Rule: ∇(ƒ - g) = ∇ƒ - ∇g 3. Constant Multiple Rule: ∇(kƒ) = k∇ƒ (any number k) 4. Product Rule: ∇(ƒg) = ƒ∇g + g∇ƒ"},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.40395,"w":0.24642,"h":0.06906,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"5. Quotient Rule: ∇aƒ"},{"x":0.59183,"y":0.40395,"w":0.12528,"h":0.06906,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"gb = g∇ƒ - ƒ∇g"},{"x":0.74784,"y":0.40947,"w":0.13305,"h":0.02421,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Scalar multipliers on left of gradients"},{"x":0.66933,"y":0.42114,"w":0.05601,"h":0.03349,"fs":0.0243,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"g2"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.48754,"w":0.34331,"h":0.01687,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Example 5 We illustrate two of the rules with"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.51467,"w":0.41479,"h":0.0914,"fs":0.0243,"n":7,"k":0,"t":"ƒ(x, y) = x - y g(x, y) = 3y ∇ƒ = i - j ∇g = 3j. We have 1. ∇(ƒ - g) = ∇(x - 4y) = i - 4j = ∇ƒ - ∇g Rule 2 2. ∇(ƒg) = ∇(3xy - 3y2) = 3yi + (3x - 6y)j"},{"x":0.40054,"y":0.60803,"w":0.40883,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"= 3y(i - j) + 3yj + (3x - 6y)j g∇ƒ plus terms . . ."},{"x":0.40054,"y":0.62721,"w":0.35975,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"simplified. = 3y(i - j) + (3x - 3y)j"},{"x":0.40054,"y":0.63669,"w":0.34219,"h":0.02712,"fs":0.0243,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"= 3y(i - j) + (x - y)3j = g∇ƒ + ƒ∇g Rule 4\b"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.68344,"w":0.21772,"h":0.01658,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Functions of Three Variables"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.70779,"w":0.4244,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"For a differentiable function ƒ(x, y, z) and a unit vector u = u1"},{"x":0.74869,"y":0.70779,"w":0.04409,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"i + u2"},{"x":0.79292,"y":0.70779,"w":0.04399,"h":0.01723,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"j + u3"},{"x":0.32331,"y":0.70794,"w":0.59302,"h":0.03009,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"k in space, we have"},{"x":0.5343,"y":0.74817,"w":0.05869,"h":0.02672,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"∇ƒ = 0ƒ"},{"x":0.57909,"y":0.74817,"w":0.12298,"h":0.03335,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"0x i + 0ƒ 0y j + 0ƒ 0z k"},{"x":0.32333,"y":0.78514,"w":0.02362,"h":0.01427,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"and"},{"x":0.4859,"y":0.80572,"w":0.1297,"h":0.02927,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"Duƒ = ∇ƒ # u = 0ƒ"},{"x":0.60171,"y":0.80572,"w":0.14818,"h":0.03335,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"0x u1 + 0ƒ 0y u2 + 0ƒ 0z u3."},{"x":0.45976,"y":0.83326,"w":0.31626,"h":0.0776,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"Duƒ = ∇ƒ # u = 0 ∇ƒ 0 0 u 0 cos u = 0 ∇ƒ 0 cos u,"},{"x":0.32333,"y":0.8427,"w":0.41551,"h":0.01427,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The directional derivative can once again be written in the form"},{"x":0.3233,"y":0.88874,"w":0.5929,"h":0.04729,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"so the properties listed earlier for functions of two variables extend to three variables. At any given point, ƒ increases most rapidly in the direction of ∇ƒ and decreases most rap- idly in the direction of -∇ƒ. In any direction orthogonal to ∇ƒ, the derivative is zero."}]}